WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.201
-We're here with Chris Hayes.

00:00:02.201 --> 00:00:04.037
Second show after the end
of the writers' strike.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.604
We're seeing a real
interesting moment

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:07.473
with labor organizing
in America.

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:09.909
Obviously, SAG-AFTRA
is still on strike.

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:14.214
We saw Biden on the picket line
with the autoworkers.

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:16.783
You know, I think we all think
about strikes as, you know,

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:19.085
wages, benefits,
working conditions,

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:22.455
but what else about this moment
do you think is pushing

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.556
what we see is a very
strong labor movement

00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:25.792
in a way we hadn't
for a long time?

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:27.093
-You know, there's a few things.

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.328
One, I think -- You know,
Alex Press is a really good

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:31.230
labor reporter, who's been
writing for Jacobin.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.698
I had her on the podcast,
you know,

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:34.367
talked about so much
of this stuff

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.803
ends up coming about -- things
that are deeper than wages.

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:39.038
It's about
autonomy and dignity.

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:41.640
And I've talked to
labor organizers for many years.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.210
I used to be largely
a labor reporter.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.112
And I had an organizer
say something similar to me,

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:48.647
that this stuff is always
deeper than wages.

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:50.550
I mean, wages and healthcare
and benefits

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.652
and work hours
and stuff like that matter,

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:55.888
but when you're doing something
as risky as a strike,

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:58.324
it's because some sense
of your dignity

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.259
and your autonomy
as a human being

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.128
has been
fundamentally insulted.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:05.364
And I think one of the things
is that we've seen COVID

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:07.400
provoked
an enormous reassessment

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.435
for a lot of people
about what your value

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:10.837
and what your worth is,

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.872
about who values you
and who doesn't value,

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:14.541
whether the boss does or not,

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:16.509
about what you want
to do with your life.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:19.612
And I think that, combined
with very tight labor markets,

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:21.114
which I think have
sort of helped,

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:24.250
and, also,
I think this real seeding

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.719
of kind of left labor,
liberal ideas

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.487
that were the product

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:31.690
of the discrediting
of American global capitalism

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.292
that happened
after the Great Recession.

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:34.693
And we've seen that
grow and grow and grow.

00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:36.729
All that's kind of coming
together in this moment

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.998
where you have more
strike action this year

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:41.333
than we've literally seen
in decades.

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.603
-I also think, you know,
the employers

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.272
sometimes forget that
once, you know,

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.041
these unions go on strike,
once these guilds go on strike,

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:50.910
they've already shown
solidarity,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.847
and they underestimate
how much harder it is

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:57.817
to get them back to work
without you making a concession.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.352
Because they don't want
to leave their jobs.

00:02:00.352 --> 00:02:01.821
They don't want
to be out of work.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.389
And when they make
that sacrifice

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:06.626
and they put, you know,
their families

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:07.960
at a disadvantage going out,

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.495
you've got to then
bring something to the table.

00:02:09.495 --> 00:02:11.130
So don't let them go on strike.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.932
That's the first thing
you should try very hard to do.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.266
-I totally agree with that.

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:16.435
And I think one of the things
you see is that

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:19.572
because strikes had become
so rare in America

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.574
over a long period of time,
there was disbelief.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:23.776
I mean, the WGA
is a little bit different

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.278
because it has been
such a militant union.

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:26.880
Obviously,
they had a strike in 2008.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.982
-Yep.
-But, again, that's 15 years.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.850
A lot has changed in 15 years.
-Yeah.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.219
-So, I think that
sort of a reminder of this --

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:36.022
I mean, a strike
is a great tool for unions

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:37.924
if you don't have
to use it, right?

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:42.328
And so I do hope that these
strikes we're seeing this year

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.430
have broader effects
throughout the economy

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.498
in how folks in management
are thinking about that.

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:48.067
-You have --
You mentioned your podcast.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.836
You have a wonderful podcast
and you're doing it live.

00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:53.072
Here we go. Yeah.
"Why Is This Happening?"

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.740
And do you enjoy doing it
in front of a live audience?

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.208
-Oh, I love it. I love it.

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:57.610
I love --
I'm a theater kid at heart.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.280
I like --
-Yeah. I mean --

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.147
-Yeah, you really are. I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:04.683
I feel like people don't see
enough of that side of you.

00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:06.485
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:07.987
I like that you were
a theater kid

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:09.489
and your theater director
was like,

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:11.558
"You should go out and talk
about political issues."

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:13.158
-Yeah.

00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:14.594
-You're like, "I think I'd
make a good Hamlet."

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.629
They're like,
"No talk about labor laws."

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.964
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.964 --> 00:03:21.000
-This is what I ended up doing.
It's so funny.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.602
-But, yeah, it's got
Chicago, Philadelphia, New York.

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:24.804
It's a wonderful podcast.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.338
And you do, like,
really drill down on an issue

00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:27.906
in a way that's helpful.

00:03:27.906 --> 00:03:29.575
I love listening to it.
-Thank you very much.

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:31.544
Yeah, we're really enjoying it.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.680
-You're not just a theater kid.
You're a real outdoorsman.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.183
And you posted
this photo recently,

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.752
because a lot of people know
you from TV, and there you are.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.088
So, someone else cut down these
trees, and then you walked over?

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:44.891
-That's exactly right.
That's exactly right.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:47.426
-Well, I know --
So, you did cut down the tree.

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:49.762
-There was a lot of
hair and makeup just off set.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:50.796
-Right, yeah.

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:52.398
-Right before
we set up the shot.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.101
-Yeah. They were like -- They're
misting you to make it look --

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:57.002
-It was below the line workers
during the strike.

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:58.604
I was like,
"I got a gig for you."

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:01.774
-So, but you recently -- Who was
it that addressed the fact

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.709
that you maybe weren't
what they expected to see --

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:05.111
-So, my wife took this picture.

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:06.845
We have a place upstate,
and I was chopping up

00:04:06.845 --> 00:04:08.380
some trees,
as you can see there.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.883
And I posted it
because I was in a bagel shop,

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.318
and this guy
was serving me a bagel.

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.386
And he sort of looks at me
and he goes,

00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:16.088
"Are you the guy from TV?"
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:17.189
He's like, "You're Chris Hayes.
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:19.124
He goes, "You know,
you look like

00:04:19.124 --> 00:04:21.928
a little accountant on TV,

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:23.663
but in person,
you look like a ballplayer."

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:24.697
-Wow!

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:25.998
-I was like,
"Well, thank you, buddy."

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:27.133
-That's really nice.
-I'm trying.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:28.601
-Yeah.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:32.204
-Just trying to remind everyone
I'm not a little accountant.

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:34.006
That's why I had my wife
take a picture of me

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.140
chopping down trees.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.909
-Cutting down trees,
going to get my bagel --

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:37.943
tough-guy stuff.

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:39.412
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:42.014
-I want to give you --
You're very issue-based,

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:45.617
and I want to give you an issue
to make an argument for,

00:04:45.617 --> 00:04:47.620
because I know you feel
very passionate about this.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.322
As passionate as you feel
about labor laws

00:04:50.322 --> 00:04:51.591
and the plight
of the working man,

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.827
you feel strongest that
minivans get a bad rap.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:58.497
-Minivans. Thank you!
Who -- Thank you!

00:04:58.497 --> 00:05:01.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I'm gonna give you 60 seconds.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.302
We're going to put it
on the board.

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:05.304 line:0%
Make a case, Chris Hayes,
for minivans. Go.

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:06.772 line:0%
-Alright, first negative case.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.374 line:0%
The crossover is dead.
It looks terrible.

00:05:09.374 --> 00:05:12.678 line:0%
Everyone's driving
some, like, knock-off S.U.V.

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:14.046 line:0%
Everyone -- What are you doing?

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:16.482 line:0%
You're dropping your kids off
in a Jeep?

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.385 line:0%
You're not going to war.
You're not driving on sand.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:21.019 line:0%
You're not doing anything
you need a Jeep for.

00:05:21.019 --> 00:05:24.490 line:0%
You're driving to third grade
to drop your kids off.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.726 line:0%
You do not need
an enormous S.U.V.

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:28.560 line:0%
But if, like me,
you have three kids,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.532 line:0%
if you're a family man,
a family woman, you need space.

00:05:33.532 --> 00:05:37.036 line:0%
The van, the minivan,
in all its beautiful glory,

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:41.073 line:0%
is the sublime synthesis
of form following function.

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:42.141 line:0%
-Oh, my God.

00:05:42.141 --> 00:05:46.145 line:0%
-When you drive
around in a minivan,

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.214 line:0%
you're saying to the world

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:51.984 line:0%
and using the correct tool
for the correct task,

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.453 line:0%
which is trucking
your kids around

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:57.356 line:0%
to endless things
they have to go do

00:05:57.356 --> 00:05:59.058 line:0%
and not pretending
you're driving down a Jeep

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.392 line:0%
where you're going to war.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.594 line:0%
-There you go. Chris Hayes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:02.594 --> 00:06:04.863
-So sad.

00:06:04.863 --> 00:06:09.835
It's so sad to see you in
the pocket of the minivan lobby.

00:06:09.835 --> 00:06:11.470
-That's all --
I'm not sponsored.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.039
I just want to be clear.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.306
-As a newsman, there's no --

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:16.275
-Yeah. I will say, I mean,
a minivan is great.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:18.978
When you -- When a friend
comes over with a minivan

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:20.412
and the door opens
and the kids get out

00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:21.714
and you don't have to help them,
I'm like, "Why?"

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:23.181
-You're not -- This move?
-This move sucks.

00:06:23.181 --> 00:06:24.350
-We don't need that move.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:25.751
-Yeah, I leave my kids
in the car now.

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:27.453
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:29.488
-I drop them off at school
and then I'm like,

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:30.690
"If you can't get out,
I'm taking you home."

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.892
-"Get out."
-You guys, that's Chris Hayes.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:35.828
-"All In With Chris Hayes" airs
weeknights at 8:00 on MSNBC.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:36.895
Tickets are still available to

00:06:36.895 --> 00:06:38.230
the "Why Is This Happening?"
tour.

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:39.565
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

